+; DADAO cancellable-syscall entry (musl cancellation protocol).
+;
+; long __syscall_cp_asm(volatile void *ptr, long nr,
+;                       long u, long v, long w, long x, long y, long z);
+;
+; ptr points at the calling thread's `cancel` flag (pthread_impl.h:38,
+; `volatile int cancel`).  The contract this file implements:
+;
+;   * musl's cancellation protocol (src/thread/pthread_cancel.c): the
+;     cancellation signal handler redirects a thread that is inside
+;     [__cp_begin, __cp_end) to __cp_cancel instead of restarting its syscall,
+;     so the flag test and the trap must be contiguous and must not touch the
+;     stack -- that is why this is assembly at all (riscv64/syscall_cp.s is the
+;     same shape);
+;   * the DADAO syscall convention (contract-syscall.md): number in rd15,
+;     arguments routed by TYPE into the rd bank (data, from rd16) and the rb
+;     bank (address/pointer, from rb16) -- the §3 worked example is
+;     read(int fd, void *buf, size_t count) => fd rd16, buf rb16, count rd17;
+;     result in rd8, error as a negative rd8 (§4, so __syscall_ret() is
+;     untouched).
+;
+; ## Argument routing (the one non-obvious part)
+;
+; musl passes the six syscall arguments as plain `syscall_arg_t` (long), so
+; their pointer-ness is already gone by the time this stub runs; the syscall
+; NUMBER is the only thing left that says which positions belong to the rb
+; bank.  The dispatch below therefore mirrors __dadao_argmask() in
+; arch/dadao/syscall_arch.h number for number (same masks, same slots) for the
+; syscalls musl actually routes through __syscall_cp/syscall_cp on a 64-bit
+; arch -- the cancellable subset, which is the only thing that can reach this
+; entry point (../__syscall_cp.c).  Each label is one mask class, and the
+; classes used here are exactly the masks those numbers have in that table:
+;
+;   mask 0x00 close, fcntl, fsync, fdatasync            (no address argument)
+;   mask 0x01 getrandom, msync, rt_sigsuspend
+;   mask 0x02 read, write, readv, writev, pread64, pwrite64, preadv, pwritev,
+;            preadv2, pwritev2, openat, msgrcv, msgsnd, epoll_pwait,
+;            mq_timedsend, sendmmsg, sendmsg, recvmsg
+;   mask 0x03 nanosleep
+;   mask 0x04 ioctl
+;   mask 0x06 accept, accept4, connect
+;   mask 0x07 rt_sigtimedwait
+;   mask 0x0a wait4
+;   mask 0x0c clock_nanosleep
+;   mask 0x0d ppoll
+;   mask 0x12 sendto, recvmmsg
+;   mask 0x14 waitid
+;   mask 0x19 futex
+;   mask 0x1a mq_timedreceive
+;   mask 0x32 recvfrom
+;   mask 0x3e pselect6
+;
+; (sendto/recvfrom are the two that need three address slots: buffer, sockaddr,
+; and recvfrom's socklen pointer -- the 0x32 class.)
+;
+; INVARIANT (same one registered on __dadao_argmask, gated by
+; tools/qemu/check_syscall_abi.py): every number dispatched here must have the
+; same mask as the C table, and every label used here must exist.  A number
+; served with wrongly-placed arguments would read garbage, not fail loudly.
+;
+; ## Register safety of the shuffles
+;
+; Entry: rb16 = ptr, rd16 = nr, rd17..rd22 = u..z.  Each block issues all
+; address-bank copies first (they only read rd17..rd22, which no earlier move
+; has touched), then the data-bank copies in ascending source order; a data
+; copy's destination rd(16+di) is always lower than both its source rd(17+i)
+; and the sources still pending, so nothing is clobbered.  rd2rd forbids
+; overlapping ranges (SimRISC-02 §2), which is another reason the moves are one
+; register at a time.  Scratch used: rd8 (the return register, reloaded by the
+; trap) and rd9 (constant for the compare chain) -- both caller-saved
+; (contract-abi.md
+; §4.5 saves only rd32-rd63).
+;
+; ## Known boundary (registered in INFRA-085t / ISS-242)
+;
+; The redirect above is only observable if the cancellation signal path works
+; (signal delivery + ucontext PC rewrite).  The DADAO port's signal frame is
+; still [OPEN] (arch/dadao/bits/signal.h), so today this stub is reachable and
+; correct but cancellation cannot actually fire: __cp_begin/__cp_end exist for
+; the handler that will use them, and __cancel() is still reached through
+; __syscall_cp_c()'s -EINTR path.
+;
+; musl builds `.s` through its plain CC_CMD path (cc treats `.s` as assembler
+; input; ADD_CFI is off for dadao -- there is no tools/add-cfi.dadao.awk), and
+; DADAO assembly uses `;` for comments ('#' is reserved for cpp, Toolchain-01
+; §2.2).
